 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "WEST: Kirkby Stephen and the Lakes, with Wainwright's blessing. EAST: Keld, Reeth and Richmond. Remember to pace yourself, it is a hundred and ninety-two miles."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "Alfred Wainwright devised this walk and published it in 1973: 192 miles from St Bees on the Irish Sea to Robin Hood's Bay on the North Sea, across three National Parks, the Lakes, the Dales and the North York Moors. It is not an official National Trail, but walkers by tradition dip their boots at one end and toss a pebble in at the other.",
   "plaque": "w55e_coast_to_coast",
   "title": "The Coast to Coast Walk"
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "A line of big stone cairns on the skyline above Kirkby Stephen, 662 metres up. Nobody knows who built them or why. Theories include boundary markers and a trick to make passing armies think more men were watching. Walkers go round them by different routes in the wet, which is part of why the path is so wide.",
   "plaque": "w55e_nine_standards",
   "title": "Nine Standards Rigg"
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "KELD. A hamlet at the head of Swaledale, where the Pennine Way and the Coast to Coast cross. Kisdon Force is just up the beck. Pack a flask."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "For centuries Swaledale and Arkengarthdale were among the great lead mining dales of England. Hundreds of men and their families lived off the ore, dressing it, smelting it and carting it to Richmond. When prices collapsed in the 1870s many families left for the coalfields, or America, and the barns and the long walls of the hay meadows are what they left.",
   "plaque": "w55e_swaledale_lead",
   "title": "Lead Mining in Swaledale"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "HAY MEADOWS. Cut late, in July, so that the flowers seed first. Please keep to the path or Farmer Atkinson will have a word."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "Begun about 1071 by Alan Rufus, a Breton lord who had fought at Hastings, as one of the first Norman castles in the north. The great keep, over thirty metres tall, was added about a century later. It stands high above the River Swale and is one of the best preserved early castles in England.",
   "plaque": "w55e_richmond_castle",
   "title": "Richmond Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 74,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "EAST: the North York Moors, the Cleveland Way and Robin Hood's Bay, with a pebble to throw in. WEST: Swaledale and Wainwright's sandwiches."
  }
 ],
